WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:01.810
- The reason I'm passionate
about explainability

00:00:01.810 --> 00:00:05.823
is because as a creator of
these kinds of AI systems,

00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:09.760
you know, there is responsibility

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:13.633
that we have in sort of figuring out

00:00:13.633 --> 00:00:15.040
when they might go wrong.

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:17.330
A lot of our lives today are governed

00:00:17.330 --> 00:00:20.810
by these narrow AI systems
that already work quite well

00:00:20.810 --> 00:00:24.013
in the narrow domain in
which they are built.

00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:26.250
But how do we govern them?

00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:27.860
And I think that's an important problem

00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:30.020
for our age that we need to try and solve.

00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:32.610
So I believe there are these
five interesting questions

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:34.340
that we have to begin to answer,

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:36.610
which is, is the input data biased?

00:00:36.610 --> 00:00:40.420
So if the input to a machine
learning system is biased,

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:41.950
or if it's incorrect,

00:00:41.950 --> 00:00:44.330
or if it has a blind
spot in a certain area,

00:00:44.330 --> 00:00:47.100
how do we detect it and how do we fix it?

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:49.410
The second is, is a model
making systematic errors?

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:51.790
So is it the case that, you know,

00:00:51.790 --> 00:00:53.450
whenever it's raining outside,

00:00:53.450 --> 00:00:54.920
the model's just showing me

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:56.710
weird news on Google news,

00:00:56.710 --> 00:00:57.543
that doesn't happen,

00:00:57.543 --> 00:00:59.960
but that's the kind of thing
you would like to know.

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:03.280
How certain are we about the model

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:04.790
in the regime of the input data?

00:01:04.790 --> 00:01:07.170
So, I have a model, for example,

00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:08.530
a mortgage model at a bank,

00:01:08.530 --> 00:01:10.270
and it's making decisions.

00:01:10.270 --> 00:01:14.340
But a new type of lender has
started applying for my model.

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:18.060
Maybe because there's an
economic shift or a social shift.

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:20.620
If the old model is not valid anymore,

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:24.690
How do I know that it's actually
making good calls or not?

00:01:24.690 --> 00:01:26.800
Can you explain the
logic behind the model?

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:28.530
So generally, the answer is no.

00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:30.310
So but then what can you do?

00:01:30.310 --> 00:01:32.330
What's the next best thing you can do?

00:01:32.330 --> 00:01:34.760
And how do we detect
regimes we have missing data

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:36.100
and how do we gather it?

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:40.130
So let me give you a few
examples of these problems.

00:01:40.130 --> 00:01:42.370
The first one is the input data biased?

00:01:42.370 --> 00:01:46.500
This is a generally
pretty depressing study.

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:49.840
The data is available on CDC.

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:51.645
And so what this data is,

00:01:51.645 --> 00:01:53.660
you know for every hospital visit,

00:01:53.660 --> 00:01:56.180
when somebody goes into triage in the ER,

00:01:56.180 --> 00:01:58.000
you know there is that model that's done

00:01:58.000 --> 00:01:59.830
to figure out how acute is this.

00:01:59.830 --> 00:02:02.513
Should we look at it right
away, or can they wait?

00:02:03.969 --> 00:02:05.830
And so that's called the triage model.

00:02:05.830 --> 00:02:10.430
And what you see is on
top the same network

00:02:10.430 --> 00:02:11.690
of the same data, it's colored

00:02:11.690 --> 00:02:14.400
by the predicted mortality of the model.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:15.820
And at the bottom it's colored

00:02:15.820 --> 00:02:18.490
by the actual mortality that you see.

00:02:18.490 --> 00:02:20.350
So you will see a couple
of things in this,

00:02:20.350 --> 00:02:23.460
the first thing that you can
see is that generally speaking,

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:25.690
the model does a fine enough job right?

00:02:25.690 --> 00:02:29.380
Generally speaking we'll see
more band mortality towards

00:02:29.380 --> 00:02:30.660
the right journey the model

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:33.940
is making those predictions it's fine.

00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:35.260
The second thing that you will notice

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:37.670
is that the model is very optimistic.

00:02:37.670 --> 00:02:39.790
Right, the model is very
regular and very optimistic.

00:02:39.790 --> 00:02:42.250
There are lots of places
here that are certainly

00:02:42.250 --> 00:02:44.080
at the bottom yellowish,
that the model is.

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:46.113
Yeah, it's blue, it's happy, it's fine.

00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:49.857
The one particular area
that I want to point out

00:02:49.857 --> 00:02:54.480
is circle up here which is the model says

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:56.210
it's totally fine no problem

00:02:56.210 --> 00:02:58.720
and those people all end
up basically not surviving.

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:01.440
They are experience so
the question is why?

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:04.350
It turned out that these
people were delirious enough

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:07.120
that they couldn't answer a
few questions on the survey.

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:09.230
Some missing data was causing

00:03:09.230 --> 00:03:11.470
this problem in the model, right?

00:03:11.470 --> 00:03:14.210
So this is an example
of how do we detect bias

00:03:14.210 --> 00:03:16.190
in the input data to the models

00:03:16.190 --> 00:03:18.780
where we can then go
hopefully correct it either

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:22.143
in some human way or process
or in some systematic way.

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:27.420
Second example is the model
making systematic errors.

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:31.983
This is an example from a fighter jet.

00:03:32.910 --> 00:03:34.660
If you've read the news recently,

00:03:34.660 --> 00:03:39.280
the F30 and F16 programs
aren't doing amazingly well.

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:44.280
And one of the major reasons
is that a lot of pilots

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:46.570
in these systems are
having hypoxia events.

00:03:46.570 --> 00:03:48.950
And what that means is when
they're flying the plane

00:03:48.950 --> 00:03:51.360
all of a sudden they're
they become unconscious

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:52.220
and when they we know,

00:03:52.220 --> 00:03:53.540
when they eventually land,

00:03:53.540 --> 00:03:56.110
it turns out that they had
a severe lack of oxygen

00:03:56.110 --> 00:03:57.310
for a small amount of time

00:03:57.310 --> 00:04:00.290
that led them to becoming unconscious.

00:04:00.290 --> 00:04:03.400
Now obviously this is not lost on anybody

00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:07.070
not on Lockheed, not on Boeing,
not on the Air Force itself.

00:04:07.070 --> 00:04:08.920
So the question is you know,

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:10.270
what are they doing about it.

00:04:10.270 --> 00:04:12.140
And to their credit they've installed lots

00:04:12.140 --> 00:04:13.980
and lots and lots of sensors in the plane.

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:15.730
So in fact, a plane today collects

00:04:15.730 --> 00:04:17.990
about a terabyte of data every minute

00:04:17.990 --> 00:04:20.180
from all these sensors right.

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:21.840
Now again the obvious problem,

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:23.320
when you collect a lot of data

00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:25.500
is how do you know when,

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:26.950
you know how do you know that

00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:29.450
when do you know the
data is actually pointing

00:04:29.450 --> 00:04:30.930
to something useful or not.

00:04:30.930 --> 00:04:32.620
And similar to the compliance thing

00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:33.940
that I talked about earlier,

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:38.090
in this case 99% of those
cases are just false alerts.

00:04:38.090 --> 00:04:40.520
They are alerts that happen,

00:04:40.520 --> 00:04:42.880
where you alert on every single variable.

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:44.620
So for example the thrust of the plane

00:04:44.620 --> 00:04:45.760
is too—I'm making this up—

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:47.710
but the thrust of the plane is too high.

00:04:47.710 --> 00:04:49.120
So you are alerting on it.

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:51.570
But it's not really meaningful.

00:04:51.570 --> 00:04:53.860
The things that lead to these weird events

00:04:53.860 --> 00:04:56.730
are combinations of events
that you have to find.

00:04:56.730 --> 00:04:58.440
So in this example, you know,

00:04:58.440 --> 00:04:59.900
you discover the systematic errors

00:04:59.900 --> 00:05:02.440
that the false alarm model is making,

00:05:02.440 --> 00:05:04.670
and then you expose it to,

00:05:04.670 --> 00:05:07.720
through a user experience that is
usable by a domain expert.

00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:09.750
So that a domain expert, you know,

00:05:09.750 --> 00:05:11.300
who wants to get to the root cause

00:05:11.300 --> 00:05:12.480
is able to figure out the root cause

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:14.900
and fix the problem or root cause.

00:05:14.900 --> 00:05:18.113
So this is the example in
figuring out systematic errors.

00:05:19.220 --> 00:05:21.430
The third is how certain
are we about the model

00:05:21.430 --> 00:05:23.640
in the input regime of the data?

00:05:23.640 --> 00:05:25.537
So, we've created a model

00:05:25.537 --> 00:05:29.120
and so this case, this is a
financial stress test model.

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:30.330
And we want to figure out

00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:32.170
which variables are the most important

00:05:32.170 --> 00:05:33.430
and just speeding up a little bit

00:05:33.430 --> 00:05:35.520
just looking at the time here.

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:37.100
So I'll go quickly through this.

00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:38.060
But again the case here

00:05:38.060 --> 00:05:40.490
is that for every time you
get an input to the model,

00:05:40.490 --> 00:05:42.800
you can place it on the model

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:45.350
that you created in the
back end, this topological map.

00:05:45.350 --> 00:05:48.090
And you can figure out: is it a region

00:05:48.090 --> 00:05:49.530
of high certainty or not?

00:05:49.530 --> 00:05:51.250
So at least you can attach estimates

00:05:51.250 --> 00:05:53.590
to every prediction that
you make individually.

00:05:53.590 --> 00:05:55.150
And say that hey, if I'm recommending

00:05:55.150 --> 00:05:56.770
this news article to somebody,

00:05:56.770 --> 00:05:58.230
you know, what is my estimate

00:05:58.230 --> 00:06:00.270
that I will be correct in this prediction?

00:06:00.270 --> 00:06:01.930
So I think it's a really important ability

00:06:01.930 --> 00:06:03.430
for models to have to do that.

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:07.210
Finally, the second last example here

00:06:07.210 --> 00:06:09.150
is how do we explain the
logic behind the model?

00:06:09.150 --> 00:06:11.220
So this is a picture of Alvin,

00:06:11.220 --> 00:06:12.830
for people in the machine
learning community,

00:06:12.830 --> 00:06:14.710
this was the first time we built kind

00:06:14.710 --> 00:06:16.083
of a self driving machine.

00:06:17.010 --> 00:06:18.560
And it worked pretty well actually.

00:06:18.560 --> 00:06:21.190
Right, it worked amazingly well,

00:06:21.190 --> 00:06:23.300
for the time; it had a camera system

00:06:23.300 --> 00:06:25.343
and a cell drive system and all that.

00:06:26.600 --> 00:06:28.273
It turned out that you know,

00:06:28.273 --> 00:06:30.300
they built this over the fall,

00:06:30.300 --> 00:06:31.850
they built it really really quickly.

00:06:31.850 --> 00:06:32.770
And when they tested it

00:06:32.770 --> 00:06:34.950
on the first day of
summer, it didn't work.

00:06:34.950 --> 00:06:37.723
So the question was why,
why is it not working?

00:06:38.610 --> 00:06:40.684
And it turned out that
the model had learned

00:06:40.684 --> 00:06:44.930
to basically see the sky as being gray.

00:06:44.930 --> 00:06:46.090
So if the sky was blue,

00:06:46.090 --> 00:06:47.790
the model was like what just happened?

00:06:47.790 --> 00:06:50.663
You know, I don't know what to do anymore.

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:55.047
So today, in our image detection systems,

00:06:55.047 --> 00:06:57.100
we run very more complicated models.

00:06:57.100 --> 00:06:59.930
So this is a deep learning model

00:06:59.930 --> 00:07:02.690
for people who've seen the
news recently deep learning

00:07:02.690 --> 00:07:05.530
is making all sorts of waves
in perception problems,

00:07:05.530 --> 00:07:07.290
things like image classification,

00:07:07.290 --> 00:07:09.370
you know, text and sound and so on.

00:07:09.370 --> 00:07:11.290
And in all these perception problems,

00:07:11.290 --> 00:07:12.570
you know, when you learn these models,

00:07:12.570 --> 00:07:14.500
they're essentially
huge arrays of numbers.

00:07:14.500 --> 00:07:15.750
That's what a model is these days,

00:07:15.750 --> 00:07:17.810
like these deep learning models.

00:07:17.810 --> 00:07:20.160
And they require a large
amount of data to train

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:21.780
and there is no easy explanation

00:07:21.780 --> 00:07:22.950
you know, so if somebody said,

00:07:22.950 --> 00:07:24.590
why did this model do this?

00:07:24.590 --> 00:07:25.760
What am I going to say?

00:07:25.760 --> 00:07:27.370
Will I say that the computer knows

00:07:27.370 --> 00:07:29.040
how to multiply numbers correctly?

00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:30.500
So hence it must be correct?

00:07:30.500 --> 00:07:32.240
Because that's the best I can say today

00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:34.110
is the model learned these numbers,

00:07:34.110 --> 00:07:35.690
and the computer knows how to multiply.

00:07:35.690 --> 00:07:37.973
So you know, that's it.

00:07:39.410 --> 00:07:40.980
We think there's a better way.

00:07:40.980 --> 00:07:42.480
So this is an example

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:44.420
where you use topological data analysis

00:07:44.420 --> 00:07:46.330
to introspect into the model.

00:07:46.330 --> 00:07:49.060
What you do is you take
those large weight matrices

00:07:49.060 --> 00:07:49.893
that you've learned,

00:07:49.893 --> 00:07:53.090
you throw it into the topological
data analysis framework,

00:07:53.090 --> 00:07:54.600
and it helps you figure out

00:07:54.600 --> 00:07:56.680
what are the base patterns
that the model has learned

00:07:56.680 --> 00:07:59.210
and how are they connected to each other?

00:07:59.210 --> 00:08:01.970
In fact, it also allows you
to speed up the training.

00:08:01.970 --> 00:08:02.930
So there's a problem

00:08:02.930 --> 00:08:05.190
in machine learning
called sample efficiency.

00:08:05.190 --> 00:08:08.020
How much data do you need to train a model

00:08:08.020 --> 00:08:10.680
so that you like its result?

00:08:10.680 --> 00:08:12.350
Sample efficiency is a huge problem.

00:08:12.350 --> 00:08:14.090
And so topological data analysis

00:08:14.090 --> 00:08:16.820
at least in this perception
visual problem allows

00:08:16.820 --> 00:08:18.360
you to speed up your training time

00:08:18.360 --> 00:08:21.500
by about 4X by giving it
some topological information

00:08:21.500 --> 00:08:24.164
about the base underlying data.

00:08:24.164 --> 00:08:26.747
 

